    "kicker": "Personal",
    "summary": "I wanted a personal website again, which is a slightly odd thing to want in 2026. Everything I make already has somewhere to go — photographs to one app, songs to another, work to a third. Each of those places is happy to host what I make on the condition that it looks like everything else there.",
    "html": "<p>I wanted a personal website again, which is a slightly odd thing to want in 2026. Everything I make already has somewhere to go — photographs to one app, songs to another, work to a third. Each of those places is happy to host what I make on the condition that it looks like everything else there.</p>\n<p>So this is the other thing. A place where the photographs are large, the songs are unfinished, and nothing is optimised for anyone's feed.</p>\n<h3>What goes here</h3>\n<p>Four things, roughly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Photographs I took and wanted to keep</li>\n<li>Music made with too many pedals and not enough endings</li>\n<li>Software I built because the alternative was doing the task by hand</li>\n<li>Notes, occasionally, when something is worth writing down</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The common thread is that none of it is finished. That used to feel like a reason not to publish. I've decided it's the reason to.</p>\n<blockquote>The work you'd only show once it's done is usually the work you never show.</blockquote>\n<h3>How it's built</h3>\n<p>Plain HTML, CSS and about a hundred lines of JavaScript. No framework, no build step beyond a script that reads what's in a folder. If I want a new photograph on the site I put the file in <code>images/</code> and the site grows by one photograph.</p>\n<p>That constraint is doing a lot of work. It means adding to this place is easier than not adding to it, which is the only way a personal site survives past its first month.</p>"
